# Cookies

HTTP is **stateless**: every request is independent of every other one. The browser can remember things and the server can remember things, but the protocol connecting them remembers nothing.

That is a problem as soon as anyone logs in:

```
client                                    server
  POST /login.html      ------------->
                        <-------------     "welcome back"
  GET /profile          ------------->     ...who is this?
```

Nothing in that second request says it came from the same person as the first.

**Cookies** are the fix. The server sends a small piece of data with its response; the browser stores it and sends it back with every subsequent request. The second exchange becomes:

```
client                                    server
  POST /login.html      ------------->
                        <-------------     "welcome back" + Set-Cookie
  GET /profile + cookie ------------->     this is Alice
```

The cookie itself usually holds nothing interesting: a short identifier that the server looks up in its own records. The real data stays on the server.

## The Other Use for Cookies

The same mechanism enables tracking, which is worth understanding rather than being vaguely uneasy about.

Your page tells the browser to load an image or a script from **another** server. That server's response can set a cookie of its own, and the browser stores it. Later you visit a completely different site that also loads something from that same third server, and the browser dutifully sends that cookie along.

The third server has now seen you on two unrelated sites and can join them up. Nobody had to cooperate; the browser simply followed the rules. This is what "third-party cookies" means, and it is why browsers and regulators have spent years restricting them.

## What a Cookie Looks Like

A cookie is name-value pairs and attributes, separated by semicolons. The server sends one with the **`Set-Cookie`** header:

```
Set-Cookie: userId=abc123; Max-Age=3600; HttpOnly
```

```javascript
response.writeHead(200, {
  'Content-Type': 'text/html',
  'Set-Cookie': 'exampleCookie=hello world; Path=/; Max-Age=3600'
});
```

The name and value are yours to choose. The attributes have fixed meanings:

| Attribute | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `Expires` | A date at which the cookie stops being valid |
| `Max-Age` | How many **seconds** from now the cookie lasts |
| `Domain` | Which domains the browser should send it to |
| `Path` | Which URLs on that domain it is sent for |
| `Secure` | Only send over HTTPS |
| `HttpOnly` | Hide it from JavaScript entirely |
| `SameSite` | Whether to send it on cross-site requests |

`Secure`, `HttpOnly`, and `SameSite` are the security-relevant three. `HttpOnly` in particular means a script on your page cannot read the cookie at all, which is what you want for a session identifier: if an attacker manages to run JavaScript on your page, they still cannot steal the session.

## Reading Cookies on the Server

The browser sends them back in the **`Cookie`** header:

```javascript
console.log(request.headers.cookie);   // 'exampleCookie=hello world'
```

Set that cookie once and it comes back on **every** request to the server: `/index.html`, `/other.html`, images, everything. That is the behavior that makes cookies useful and also the behavior that makes them heavy, since they ride along with requests that have no use for them.

It arrives as one string, so getting a single value means parsing it:

```{javascript autorun}
function readCookie(cookieHeader, name) {
  if (!cookieHeader) {
    return null;
  }

  for (const pair of cookieHeader.split(';')) {
    const parts = pair.trim().split('=');

    if (parts[0] === name) {
      return parts[1];
    }
  }

  return null;
}

console.log(readCookie('theme=dark; userId=abc123', 'userId'));
console.log(readCookie('theme=dark; userId=abc123', 'missing'));
console.log(readCookie(undefined, 'userId'));
```

Note the `trim()`. The pairs are separated by `; ` with a space, so splitting on `;` alone leaves the space attached to the next name.

## Deleting a Cookie

The server cannot reach into the browser and remove one. What it can do is set the same cookie again with **`Max-Age=0`**, which tells the browser it has already expired:

```javascript
response.writeHead(200, {
  'Set-Cookie': 'exampleCookie=; Path=/; Max-Age=0'
});
```

Refresh and the cookie is gone from the browser's list.

## Cookies in the Browser: `document.cookie`

Page scripts can also work with cookies, as long as they are not `HttpOnly`.

Reading gives you every visible cookie in one string, in the same format you just parsed:

```javascript
console.log(document.cookie);   // 'theme=dark; userId=abc123'
```

Writing sets **one cookie at a time**, and this is the part that surprises people. Assigning does not replace the string; it adds or updates a single entry:

```javascript
document.cookie = 'theme=dark';
document.cookie = 'lang=en; max-age=3600';

// document.cookie is now 'theme=dark; lang=en'
```

Deleting works the same way as on the server:

```javascript
document.cookie = 'theme=; max-age=0';
```

Cookies marked `HttpOnly` never appear here at all. That is the point of the attribute.

## Looking at Real Cookies

You can see all of this on any site you visit. Open the developer tools, find the storage or application section, and look at Cookies for the current domain. On a typical university page you might find:

- **`__cf_bm`**, Cloudflare's bot management cookie, used to judge whether requests are coming from a person.
- **`_ga`** and friends, Google Analytics, which is how a site owner sees how visitors move through their pages, and how the same visitor is recognized across many sites.

Almost every site you use has several, and few of them are the site's own.

Cookies and the storage APIs from the previous reading are not rivals. Cookies go to the server automatically, which is what makes them right for identifying a session; `localStorage` never leaves the browser, which is what makes it right for a theme preference. Real applications use both.

## Exercises

1. Complete `readCookie` so it returns the value for a name, and `null` when there is nothing to return.

    ```{javascript}
    function readCookie(cookieHeader, name) {
      if (!cookieHeader) {
        return null;
      }

      for (const pair of cookieHeader.split(';')) {
        // TODO: split the pair into a name and a value, ignoring any
        // surrounding spaces, and return the value if the name matches
      }

      return null;
    }

    console.log(readCookie('theme=dark; session=abc123', 'session'));
    ```
    ```+solution
    function readCookie(cookieHeader, name) {
      if (!cookieHeader) {
        return null;
      }

      for (const pair of cookieHeader.split(';')) {
        const parts = pair.trim().split('=');

        if (parts[0] === name) {
          return parts[1];
        }
      }

      return null;
    }

    console.log(readCookie('theme=dark; session=abc123', 'session'));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(readCookie('theme=dark; session=abc123', 'session')).should('equal', 'abc123').run('Return the value that follows the matching name', 'Read a cookie value');
    wrap(readCookie('theme=dark; session=abc123', 'theme')).should('equal', 'dark').run('It should work for the first cookie as well as the last', 'Read the first cookie too');
    wrap(readCookie('theme=dark; session=abc123', 'nothere')).should('equal', null).run('Return `null` when no cookie has that name', 'Handled a missing cookie');
    wrap(readCookie(undefined, 'session')).should('equal', null).run('A request with no cookies at all has no `Cookie` header, so handle a missing header', 'Handled a missing header');
    ```

2. Write `buildSetCookie`, which produces the header value a server would send.

    ```{javascript}
    function buildSetCookie(name, value, maxAgeSeconds) {
      // TODO: return a Set-Cookie value of the form
      // 'name=value; Path=/; Max-Age=seconds'
    }

    console.log(buildSetCookie('userId', 'abc123', 3600));
    console.log(buildSetCookie('userId', '', 0));
    ```
    ```+solution
    function buildSetCookie(name, value, maxAgeSeconds) {
      return name + '=' + value + '; Path=/; Max-Age=' + maxAgeSeconds;
    }

    console.log(buildSetCookie('userId', 'abc123', 3600));
    console.log(buildSetCookie('userId', '', 0));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(buildSetCookie('userId', 'abc123', 3600)).should('equal', 'userId=abc123; Path=/; Max-Age=3600').run('Join the name and value with `=`, then the attributes, separated by `; `', 'Built the header value');
    wrap(buildSetCookie('theme', 'dark', 60)).should('equal', 'theme=dark; Path=/; Max-Age=60').run('It should work for any name, value, and lifetime', 'Works in general');
    wrap(buildSetCookie('userId', '', 0)).should('equal', 'userId=; Path=/; Max-Age=0').run('With a `Max-Age` of `0` this is how a server asks the browser to delete a cookie', 'Produced a deleting header');
    ```

3. What is it about HTTP that makes cookies necessary, and how does that let an outside server track you across sites?

    ```{mcq}
    Pick the accurate account.
    correct: Correct! Because HTTP forgets, something has to carry identity between requests. And because a page can pull resources from other servers, those servers get to set and read their own cookies, letting them recognize you on every site that embeds them.
    wrong: Start with what HTTP does not do on its own, then think about who else gets to set a cookie when a page loads an image from elsewhere.
    [x] HTTP is stateless, so each request is independent; and a page loading images or scripts from an outside server lets that server set and later read its own cookie
    [ ] HTTP is stateful and tracks users already, so outside servers use cookies to get around that tracking
    [ ] HTTP is encrypted by default, so cookies are needed to recover the client's IP address
    [ ] HTTP is separate per tab, so cookies are used to link different browsers together
    ```

4. How does a server make a browser delete a cookie it previously set?

    ```{mcq}
    Which of these actually removes it?
    correct: Correct! Send the same cookie again with `Max-Age=0`. The browser treats it as already expired and discards it.
    wrong: The server cannot reach into the browser. It has to send something that the browser will act on.
    [x] Send `Set-Cookie` again for the same name with `Max-Age=0`
    [ ] Leave the `Set-Cookie` header out of the next response
    [ ] Set the cookie's `Path` to an empty string
    [ ] Run `document.cookie = null` in the server file
    ```

5. A page already has a `theme=dark` cookie, and your script runs `document.cookie = 'lang=en';`. What does `document.cookie` hold afterwards?

    ```{mcq}
    Does assigning replace the whole string?
    correct: Correct! Assigning to `document.cookie` adds or updates one cookie and leaves the rest alone, even though reading it gives you all of them at once.
    wrong: Reading and writing `document.cookie` are not symmetrical. Reading gives you everything; writing does something narrower.
    [x] Both cookies, such as `theme=dark; lang=en`
    [ ] Only `lang=en`, because the assignment replaced everything
    [ ] An empty string, because assigning clears the cookies
    [ ] Only `theme=dark`, because cookies cannot be written from JavaScript
    ```

